---
import { repositories, site } from "../data/site";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { photo } from "../lib/photos";

const credits = [
  {
    name: "Mixxx",
    href: site.mixxx,
    what: "The free, open-source DJ software the deck runs. TriMixxx is a fork of Mixxx 2.5.6; everything that makes a deck play music starts there.",
  },
  {
    name: "ttymidi",
    href: "https://github.com/usr-ein/ttymidi",
    what: "varal's serial-to-ALSA MIDI bridge, forked to carry SysEx both ways: the only thing between the controller's UART and Mixxx.",
  },
  {
    name: "ch32fun",
    href: "https://github.com/cnlohr/ch32fun",
    what: "cnlohr's bare-metal toolkit for the CH32V003, and its minichlink flasher. Every pad node runs on it.",
  },
  {
    name: "cdj2000-emulator",
    href: "https://github.com/cdj2k-revival/cdj2000-emulator",
    what: "cdj2k-revival's emulator of the CDJ-2000, which boots the player's own firmware. TriMixxx's fork puts emulated CDJ-2000NXS players on the decks' network.",
  },
  {
    name: "RAUC",
    href: "https://rauc.io",
    what: "The update framework behind the deck's A/B slots and its signed bundles.",
  },
  {
    name: "Chocolate Doom",
    href: "https://www.chocolate-doom.org",
    what: "The faithful DOOM port the deck boots into when PLAY is held, unmodified.",
  },
  {
    name: "Raspberry Pi OS, KiCad, PlatformIO",
    href: "https://www.kicad.org",
    what: "The system the deck boots, the tool every board was drawn in, and the build system of its controller firmware.",
  },
  {
    name: "The Pro DJ Link community",
    href: "https://github.com/Deep-Symmetry/dysentery",
    what: "Deep Symmetry's dysentery and the other published Pro DJ Link projects were the starting map. TriMixxx's own implementation was then written from captures of its own hardware, without their code.",
  },
] as const;

const share = await photo("2026-10-02-img-9266");
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Who makes TriMixxx, how to get in touch, the projects it builds on, and its repositories and licences."
  image={share?.data.image}
  imageAlt={share?.data.alt}
>
  <section class="wrap section section--tight">
    <div class="about">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="kicker">About</span>
        <h1>A deck, made in the open.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          TriMixxx is designed and built by {site.author}: the boards, the firmware, the Mixxx fork
          and the Pro DJ Link work. It began in February 2026 as a single circuit board, and became
          a deck that played its first gig eight months later.
        </p>
        <p class="lede">
          It is not a product, and there is nothing to buy. It is a working deck whose every layer
          is published, so that whoever wants to try an idea in the booth, whether a new control, a
          new way to sync or a new screen, has a whole deck to try it on.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="contact card" aria-labelledby="contact-title">
        <span class="label">Get in touch</span>
        <h2 id="contact-title" class="contact__title">
          Write to the deck.
        </h2>
        <p>About the project, a collaboration, an event or the press:</p>
        <p class="contact__email mono">
          <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>
        </p>
        <p>
          Bugs and code go to{" "}
          <a href={`${site.repo}/issues`} rel="noopener">
            GitHub issues
          </a>
          .
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section--line" id="why">
    <div class="section__head">
      <span class="kicker">Why</span>
      <h2>Why build a deck at all.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose why">
      <p>
        Pioneer DJ's CDJs are the industry standard. Thousands of venues already have them set up,
        and DJs expect to find them when they walk into a booth: players that read the rekordbox
        stick they brought, with an interface they all know how to use.
      </p>
      <p>
        That standard has made Pioneer a monopoly in all but name, and a monopoly holds back what
        anyone else might invent. Its players are closed to the people who own them, too: there is
        no upgrading the hardware and no adding to what it can do. I believe that whoever buys and
        owns a piece of hardware should be free to do whatever they want with it, and never be
        locked out of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also wanted to learn, for myself, every skill it takes to put a finished product together:
        mechanical design and 3D printing, PCB design, embedded electronics and firmware, C++, audio
        processing, and the rest.
      </p>
      <p>
        A CDJ is not much more complex than a very large iPod. It plays music files out of a socket;
        the jog wheel, the buttons and the screen around it are interface. By building TriMixxx in
        the open, I want to give everyone curious about it a known base to build on and to iterate
        from, and to show the problems I ran into and how I solved them, so that building something
        like it yourself looks a lot less daunting.
      </p>
      <p class="why__signature">{site.author}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section--line" id="credits">
    <div class="section__head">
      <span class="kicker">Standing on</span>
      <h2>What TriMixxx is built on.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Most of a deck already existed as free software. These are the projects it would not exist
        without.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid--2">
      {credits.map((credit) => (
        <article class="card">
          <h3>
            <a href={credit.href} rel="noopener">
              {credit.name}
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p>{credit.what}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section--line" id="source">
    <div class="section__head">
      <span class="kicker">Source</span>
      <h2>Repositories and licences.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Everything is on GitHub under free licences. The deck's own repository is GPL-3.0-or-later,
        hardware sources and documentation included; the projects it forks keep theirs.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="The repositories" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <caption class="visually-hidden">TriMixxx's repositories and their licences</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Repository</th>
            <th scope="col">What it holds</th>
            <th scope="col">Licence</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {repositories.map((repo) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                <a href={repo.href} rel="noopener">
                  {repo.name}
                </a>
              </th>
              <td class="muted">{repo.what}</td>
              <td class="mono nowrap">{repo.licence}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section--line" id="trademarks">
    <div class="section__head">
      <span class="kicker">Fine print</span>
      <h2>Trademarks and affiliation.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        TriMixxx is an independent project, with no affiliation with, sponsorship by or endorsement
        from AlphaTheta Corporation (formerly Pioneer DJ) or the Mixxx project.
      </p>
      <p>
        CDJ, rekordbox, Pro DJ Link, DJM, NXS and Pioneer DJ are trademarks of AlphaTheta
        Corporation. They appear here only to say which equipment TriMixxx works with, which
        protocol it speaks, and which decks it was measured against. Mixxx is the name of the
        independent open-source project at{" "}
        <a href={site.mixxx} rel="noopener">
          mixxx.org
        </a>
        ; TriMixxx is this project's own name, not a Mixxx product.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Pro DJ Link work is reverse engineering for interoperability, from network traffic
        between devices the author owns. Protocol facts are not copyrightable, and no code from any
        other implementation was copied. No Pioneer firmware is, or will be, in any TriMixxx
        repository.
      </p>
      <p>
        The build log shows an emulated CDJ-2000NXS at work. Its screen and lamps are drawn by
        AlphaTheta's own firmware, supplied by the player's owner; they appear only to document the
        emulator.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .about {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.5fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .about .section__head {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .contact {
    gap: 0.9rem;
    border-color: var(--lime-line);
  }

  .contact__title {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
  }

  .contact__email {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }

  .contact__email a {
    color: var(--lime);
  }

  .nowrap {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .why {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }

  .why__signature {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .why__signature::before {
    content: "— ";
  }

  @media (width <= 62rem) {
    .about {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
